/* Original teaching text and fictional examples. Public references are further reading. */
(function(root){
const lessons=[];
function add(id,group,title,intro,formula,symbols,steps,examples,deep,mistake,use,practice,answer,summary,visual){lessons.push({id,group,title,intro,formula,symbols,steps,examples,deep,mistake,use,practice,answer,summary,visual});}
add('notation','01 · Foundations','Numbers, symbols and the language of ML',
'Machine learning means adjusting a rule using examples. Mathematics lets us write that rule precisely. A feature is an input measurement, such as hours studied. A target is the quantity we want to predict. A parameter is a number the model learns, such as the importance given to hours studied. Start by separating these three roles: data comes from examples; parameters change during training; a prediction is the result of applying the current rule.',
'ŷ = wx + b     L = (1/n) Σᵢ₌₁ⁿ (ŷᵢ − yᵢ)²',
['x: an input feature','y: the observed target; ŷ (y-hat): a prediction','w: learned weight; b: learned intercept','n: number of examples; i: example index','Σ: add the expression for every listed index; L: average squared error'],
['Choose the rule before inserting numbers: multiply the input by the weight, then add the intercept.','The hat marks a predicted value. It does not mean a power. A subscript names an entry; x₂ is the second entry, while x² means x multiplied by itself.','For each training example, subtract its observed target from its prediction. Square this error so negative and positive errors do not cancel.','Add the squared errors and divide by the number of examples. This gives one loss value that training tries to reduce.'],
[{q:'Predict a score with x = 3, w = 2 and b = 1. The actual score is 8.',steps:['ŷ = 2 × 3 + 1 = 7.','Error = 7 − 8 = −1.','Squared error = (−1)² = 1.'],result:'Prediction: 7; squared error: 1.'},{q:'Two predictions are 3 and 5; targets are 2 and 7.',steps:['Squared errors are (3−2)² = 1 and (5−7)² = 4.','Average them: (1 + 4)/2 = 2.5.'],result:'Mean squared error: 2.5.'}],
'A model is a function with adjustable parameters. Training is an optimization problem: choose parameters that make a specified loss small on training data. Prediction and training are different operations. A small training loss alone does not prove the rule will work on new examples. Units also matter: if y is measured in meters, squared error is measured in square meters.',
'Do not confuse the number of examples n with the number of features d. A dataset can have 1,000 rows and only 3 input features.',
'Every later lesson uses this vocabulary. Neural networks still follow the same input → prediction → loss → parameter update pattern.',
'With w = 4, x = 2, b = −1 and y = 9, what are the prediction and squared error?',
'Prediction = 4×2−1 = 7. Error = −2. Squared error = 4.',
'Read the symbols as a recipe: data goes in, parameters control the rule, and loss measures the prediction error.','flow');
add('functions','01 · Foundations','Algebra, functions and graphs',
'A function is a rule that assigns one output to each allowed input. The allowed inputs form its domain. Its graph draws the input horizontally and the output vertically. In ML, a function can turn a feature into a score, or a whole vector into a probability. Algebra helps us rearrange these rules without changing their meaning.',
'f(x) = ax + b     (g ∘ f)(x) = g(f(x))',
['f, g: functions','a: slope, the output change per one input unit','b: output when x = 0','∘: composition, meaning apply the right function first'],
['For a straight line, b sets the starting height and a sets the tilt. Increasing x by 1 increases f(x) by a.','Solving an equation means finding an input that produces a specified output. Perform the same valid operation on both sides.','Composition chains operations. Calculate f(x) first, then give that result to g.','A nonlinear function bends or changes slope. Combining only affine layers still produces an affine rule; nonlinear activations allow neural networks to represent more complex relationships.'],
[{q:'Solve 3x + 2 = 14.',steps:['Subtract 2 from both sides: 3x = 12.','Divide both sides by 3: x = 4.','Check: 3×4 + 2 = 14.'],result:'x = 4.'},{q:'Let f(x)=2x+1 and g(u)=u². Calculate g(f(3)).',steps:['f(3)=7.','g(7)=49.','Reversing the order gives f(g(3))=f(9)=19.'],result:'g(f(3)) = 49; order matters.'}],
'A function need not be invertible. For example, x² maps both −2 and 2 to 4. Restricting its domain to nonnegative numbers makes the square-root inverse well-defined. The term linear is used loosely in ML: wx+b is technically affine when b is not zero. An exactly linear map must preserve sums and scalar multiplication.',
'Do not divide by an expression that might be zero. Do not assume f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y) for an arbitrary function.',
'Feature transformations, model prediction rules and layers are functions. Reading their graphs helps you understand behavior before training.',
'For f(x)=−2x+5, find f(3) and the input that gives output 1.',
'f(3)=−1. Solve −2x+5=1 to get x=2.',
'A function is a rule; composition builds a larger rule by connecting simpler ones.','line');
add('logs','01 · Foundations','Powers, exponentials and logarithms',
'Powers describe repeated multiplication. Exponentials put the variable in the exponent. Logarithms reverse exponentiation: log₂(8) asks which power of 2 gives 8. The answer is 3. ML uses exponentials to turn scores into positive values and logarithms to turn products of probabilities into sums that are easier to optimize.',
'aᵘaᵛ = aᵘ⁺ᵛ     ln(ab) = ln(a)+ln(b)     ln(eˣ)=x',
['e: approximately 2.71828','ln: logarithm with base e','log₂: logarithm with base 2','a,b: positive numbers in the logarithm identity'],
['A negative exponent means reciprocal: 2⁻³ = 1/8. A fractional power can mean a root: 9¹ᐟ² = 3.','An exponential is always positive for a positive base. The natural exponential has the useful derivative d(eˣ)/dx=eˣ.','A logarithm is defined for positive real inputs. ln(1)=0; probabilities below 1 have negative logarithms.','For independent observed events, multiply probabilities. Taking the log turns the multiplication into addition without changing which positive likelihood is largest.'],
[{q:'Two independent observations have probabilities 0.5 and 0.25.',steps:['Joint probability = 0.5×0.25 = 0.125.','log₂(0.125) = −3.','Equivalently, log₂(0.5)+log₂(0.25)=−1−2=−3.'],result:'Joint log probability: −3 bits.'},{q:'Compare negative log losses for probability 0.8 versus 0.2 assigned to the correct event.',steps:['−ln(0.8) ≈ 0.2231.','−ln(0.2) ≈ 1.6094.','The less confident correct prediction has the larger loss.'],result:'0.2231 versus 1.6094.'}],
'The logarithm is strictly increasing, so maximizing a likelihood is equivalent to maximizing its log. Negative log-likelihood changes maximization into minimization. Computing a product of thousands of tiny probabilities can underflow to zero; adding log probabilities avoids much of this problem. Logarithm bases change the numerical scale, not the ordering.',
'ln(a+b) is not ln(a)+ln(b). log(0) is not a finite number. Stable implementations handle extreme scores carefully.',
'Likelihood estimation, cross-entropy, softmax, entropy and numerical stability all rely on these identities.',
'Compute log₂(16) and −ln(1).',
'log₂(16)=4 because 2⁴=16. −ln(1)=0.',
'Exponentials turn sums into products; logarithms turn products into sums.','curve');
add('sets','01 · Foundations','Sets, logic and counting',
'A set is a collection of distinct items. An event in probability is a set of possible outcomes. Logic tells us which conditions an item satisfies. Counting tells us how many outcomes exist before we assign probabilities. These ideas are useful for train/test splits, class labels and reasoning about combinations.',
'|A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|     C(n,k) = n!/[k!(n−k)!]',
['|A|: number of elements in set A','∪: union, in A or B or both','∩: intersection, in both sets','!: factorial, for example 4!=4×3×2×1','C(n,k): ways to choose k items from n without order'],
['List the sample space: all possible outcomes under consideration. An event chooses some of these outcomes.','Union combines events. Intersection keeps only shared outcomes. A complement keeps outcomes outside an event.','For independent choice stages with m and n possibilities, the multiplication rule gives mn ordered pairs. This is a counting rule, separate from probabilistic independence.','Use combinations when order does not matter. Use permutations n!/(n−k)! when selecting ordered items without replacement.'],
[{q:'A contains {1,2,3}; B contains {3,4}. Count their union.',steps:['|A|=3 and |B|=2.','Their intersection is {3}, with size 1.','Union size = 3+2−1=4.'],result:'A ∪ B = {1,2,3,4}.'},{q:'Choose 2 validation items from 4 distinct items.',steps:['There are 4×3=12 ordered choices.','Each pair occurs in 2 orders.','Divide by 2: 12/2=6.'],result:'6 unordered pairs.'}],
'Universal statements say a condition holds for every item; existential statements say at least one item satisfies it. One counterexample disproves a universal statement. A finite sample with no counterexample does not prove a claim about all possible future data. This distinction matters when evaluating a trained model.',
'Do not add overlapping set sizes without subtracting the overlap. Mutually exclusive events are not the same thing as independent events.',
'Data splitting, evaluation groups, event probabilities and combinatorial search all use sets and counting.',
'How many ordered pairs can you choose without replacement from 5 items?',
'5 choices for the first item and 4 for the second: 5×4=20.',
'First decide what counts as a distinct outcome; then count it.','sets');
add('vectors','02 · Linear algebra','Vectors, tensors and shapes',
'A scalar is one number. A vector is an ordered list of numbers. A matrix is a table of numbers. In ML programming, a tensor generalizes these arrays to more axes. A vector can describe one example: [height, weight, age]. The order and meaning of each coordinate must remain consistent across the dataset.',
'x ∈ ℝᵈ     X ∈ ℝⁿˣᵈ     ‖x‖₂ = √(Σⱼ xⱼ²)',
['ℝ: real numbers','d: number of features; n: number of examples','X: a matrix with n rows and d columns','‖x‖₂: Euclidean length of x'],
['Write down the shape before computing. A batch with 8 examples and 3 features has shape (8,3).','Vector addition adds matching coordinates. Scalar multiplication multiplies every coordinate by the same number.','The Euclidean norm measures length: square each coordinate, sum, then take the square root.','A unit vector has length 1. Divide a nonzero vector by its norm to keep its direction but remove its magnitude.'],
[{q:'Find the length and unit direction of x=[3,4].',steps:['Squared length = 3²+4² = 25.','Length = √25 = 5.','Divide both entries by 5.'],result:'Norm = 5; unit vector = [0.6,0.8].'},{q:'A batch has 10 RGB images, each 32 by 32 pixels, in channel-last order.',steps:['Batch axis has 10 entries.','Two spatial axes have 32 entries each; color axis has 3.','Total scalar entries = 10×32×32×3.'],result:'Shape (10,32,32,3); 30,720 numbers.'}],
'A tensor axis is not necessarily a geometric dimension. It may represent time, batch, channel or feature. Two arrays with the same number of entries can have different meanings and incompatible shapes. Reshaping rearranges indexing; it does not automatically create a meaningful representation. Normalize only when magnitude is irrelevant or deliberately controlled.',
'The zero vector cannot be divided by its norm. Feature units can dominate distance: meters and millimeters describe the same quantity but have different numeric scales.',
'Embeddings, feature batches, images, model weights and attention activations are vectors and tensors.',
'What is the norm of [0,−5,12]?',
'√(0²+25+144)=√169=13.',
'Shape tells you how numbers are organized; a norm tells you their size.','vector');
add('dot','02 · Linear algebra','Dot products, angles and projections',
'A dot product multiplies matching vector coordinates and adds the results. You can read it as a weighted score. Geometrically it also measures alignment: vectors pointing in similar directions have a positive dot product; perpendicular vectors have zero dot product.',
'xᵀw = Σⱼ xⱼwⱼ     cos θ = (xᵀw)/(‖x‖₂‖w‖₂)     projᵤ(x) = (xᵀu)u',
['ᵀ: transpose, turning a column into a row','θ: angle between nonzero vectors','u: a unit vector in the projection formula','proj: component of x along a chosen direction'],
['Multiply each feature by its corresponding weight. Sum these products to produce one scalar.','To compare direction independently of length, divide by both vector norms. This gives cosine similarity.','To project onto a unit vector, first calculate the scalar score xᵀu, then multiply u by that score.','For a non-unit direction v, projection is [(xᵀv)/(vᵀv)]v. The denominator corrects for its length.'],
[{q:'Score x=[2,3] using w=[4,−1].',steps:['First contribution: 2×4=8.','Second contribution: 3×(−1)=−3.','Add them: 8−3=5.'],result:'Dot product: 5.'},{q:'Project [3,4] onto the horizontal unit vector [1,0].',steps:['Scalar component = 3×1+4×0=3.','Multiply [1,0] by 3.','Residual [0,4] is perpendicular to [1,0].'],result:'Projection [3,0]; residual [0,4].'}],
'Orthogonality explains least squares: at the best unconstrained linear fit, the residual is orthogonal to each column of the design matrix. Cosine similarity is invariant to positive rescaling but ignores potentially useful magnitude. A zero dot product means geometric orthogonality; it does not by itself mean two random variables are statistically independent.',
'Cosine similarity is undefined when either vector is zero. Dot product is not coordinatewise multiplication: the dot product ends with a sum.',
'Linear predictions, embedding similarity, attention scores and projection methods use dot products.',
'Find the cosine similarity between [1,0] and [1,1].',
'Dot product=1; norms=1 and √2; cosine=1/√2≈0.7071.',
'A dot product turns two vectors into one score; normalization makes that score describe direction.','vector');
add('matrices','02 · Linear algebra','Matrices and linear transformations',
'A matrix can be a dataset, but it can also describe a transformation. Multiplying a vector by a matrix produces a new vector. Each output is a dot product between a matrix row and the input. This is how a layer combines many features into several new features.',
'y = Ax     A ∈ ℝᵐˣᵈ, x ∈ ℝᵈ, y ∈ ℝᵐ     (AB)ᵢⱼ = Σₖ AᵢₖBₖⱼ',
['m: number of outputs; d: number of inputs','Aᵢₖ: entry in row i, column k','AB: apply B first, then A to a column vector','I: identity matrix, which leaves a vector unchanged'],
['Check the inner dimensions: an m×d matrix can multiply a d-entry vector.','Compute one dot product for each output row.','For matrix multiplication, pair every left row with every right column. The result keeps the outer dimensions.','Composition order matters. Rotating then stretching is generally different from stretching then rotating.'],
[{q:'Multiply A=[[2,0],[0,3]] by x=[1,2].',steps:['First row: 2×1+0×2=2.','Second row: 0×1+3×2=6.'],result:'Ax=[2,6]; horizontal scale 2, vertical scale 3.'},{q:'A batch X has shape (5,3) and weights W have shape (3,2).',steps:['The shared dimension 3 matches.','Each of 5 examples produces 2 outputs.'],result:'XW has shape (5,2).'}],
'The columns of A are the transformed coordinate basis vectors. This gives a geometric way to understand the whole transformation by inspecting only its columns. Matrix multiplication is associative, so (AB)C=A(BC), but generally not commutative: AB≠BA. Broadcasting in programming is an additional array rule, not a replacement for mathematical dimension checks.',
'Elementwise multiplication is a different operation from matrix multiplication. Do not infer the operation solely from an asterisk in an unfamiliar library.',
'Dense neural layers, batch inference, rotations, covariance calculations and feature transformations all use matrices.',
'Can a (4,3) matrix multiply a (2,4) matrix in that order?',
'No: inner sizes 3 and 2 differ. Reversing the order is valid and gives shape (2,3).',
'A matrix is a collection of weighted sums, and its shape determines which sums are possible.','matrix');
add('systems','02 · Linear algebra','Linear systems, rank and least squares',
'A linear system asks for values that satisfy several linear equations at once. It may have one solution, no exact solution, or many solutions. Rank counts independent directions represented by a matrix. When measured data cannot be fitted exactly, least squares finds the parameters with the smallest total squared residual.',
'Ax = b     minₓ ‖Ax−b‖₂²     AᵀAx = Aᵀb',
['x: unknown parameter vector','b: desired output vector','r=Ax−b: residual vector','rank: number of independent columns, equivalently independent rows'],
['Translate each equation into a matrix row.','Use row elimination or a factorization to solve the system. An inverse exists only for a square full-rank matrix.','For an inconsistent system, minimize squared residual length instead of forcing all equations to hold.','Differentiate the squared objective and set the gradient to zero: 2Aᵀ(Ax−b)=0. This gives the normal equations.'],
[{q:'Solve x+y=5 and x−y=1.',steps:['Add the equations: 2x=6, so x=3.','Insert x=3 into x+y=5.'],result:'x=3, y=2.'},{q:'Fit one constant c to observations 2 and 4.',steps:['Loss sum=(c−2)²+(c−4)².','Derivative=2(c−2)+2(c−4)=4c−12.','Set it to zero: c=3.'],result:'Best constant=3; residuals=[1,−1]; squared sum=2.'}],
'If columns are dependent, different parameter vectors may make exactly the same prediction. The Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse selects a minimum-norm least-squares solution. Forming AᵀA squares the condition number in the full-rank case, so numerical solvers often use QR or SVD instead of explicitly inverting the normal-equation matrix.',
'More equations do not guarantee a unique solution: some may repeat the same information. Avoid computing an explicit inverse just to solve Ax=b.',
'Linear regression, inverse problems and parameter identifiability depend on these ideas.',
'What is the rank of [[1,2],[2,4]]?',
'Rank 1: the second row and second column add no independent direction. The matrix is singular.',
'Rank measures independent information; least squares finds the best compromise when exact fitting is impossible.','matrix');
add('eigen','02 · Linear algebra','Eigenvalues, eigenvectors and positive matrices',
'Most vectors change direction when a matrix transforms them. An eigenvector is a special nonzero vector whose direction is preserved up to sign. Its eigenvalue says how much it is scaled. These directions help explain covariance, curvature and the long-term behavior of repeated transformations.',
'Av = λv     A = QΛQᵀ for real symmetric A     xᵀAx ≥ 0 for PSD A',
['v: nonzero eigenvector','λ (lambda): eigenvalue','Q: orthonormal eigenvectors as columns','Λ: diagonal matrix of eigenvalues','PSD: positive semidefinite'],
['Look for vectors that keep their direction after multiplication.','The scale factor along such a vector is its eigenvalue. A negative eigenvalue reverses orientation.','A real symmetric matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal eigenbasis.','A symmetric matrix is PSD exactly when all eigenvalues are nonnegative. Its quadratic form cannot be negative.'],
[{q:'For A=[[3,0],[0,1]], transform v=[1,0].',steps:['Av=[3,0].','This is 3v.','The vertical vector [0,1] is instead scaled by 1.'],result:'Eigenvalues 3 and 1 with coordinate-axis eigenvectors.'},{q:'Check xᵀAx for x=[2,1] and A=[[3,0],[0,1]].',steps:['Ax=[6,1].','xᵀAx=2×6+1×1=13.'],result:'Quadratic value 13; this A is positive definite.'}],
'Covariance matrices are symmetric PSD because any projected variance must be nonnegative. Hessian eigenvalues describe local curvature: positive values indicate upward bending, negative values downward bending. General nonsymmetric real matrices can have complex eigenvalues and may not have a complete eigenbasis, so the symmetric case should not be generalized blindly.',
'An eigenvector cannot be the zero vector. Eigenvectors can be rescaled, so their raw magnitude is not uniquely determined.',
'PCA uses covariance eigenvectors; optimization uses curvature; Markov chains use eigenstructure for stationary behavior.',
'For diagonal matrix diag(2,−1), what happens to [0,3]?',
'It becomes [0,−3], so that direction has eigenvalue −1. The matrix is not PSD.',
'Eigenvectors reveal special directions; eigenvalues tell what the transformation does along them.','vector');
add('svd','02 · Linear algebra','SVD and principal component analysis',
'Singular value decomposition separates a matrix into rotations or reflections and nonnegative stretches. It works even for rectangular matrices. PCA uses the strongest variation directions of centered data to build a smaller representation. It retains variation, which is not always the same as retaining information useful for prediction.',
'X = UΣVᵀ     Z = Xcentered Vₖ     variance ratio = σᵢ² / Σⱼσⱼ²',
['U,V: orthonormal singular directions','Σ: diagonal arrangement of nonnegative singular values σ','Vₖ: first k right singular vectors','Z: lower-dimensional coordinates'],
['Subtract the training mean from each feature. Centering makes the origin represent the average example.','Compute an SVD of the centered training matrix. Sort singular values from largest to smallest.','Keep k right singular vectors and project data onto them.','Reconstruct approximately with ZVₖᵀ and add the mean back. Discarded directions create reconstruction error.'],
[{q:'Centered points are [−1,−1] and [1,1]. Project onto u=[1,1]/√2.',steps:['First score=(−1−1)/√2=−√2.','Second score=(1+1)/√2=√2.','Multiplying each score by u reconstructs the original point.'],result:'One coordinate preserves all variation in these two points.'},{q:'Squared singular values are 9,4,1. Keep two components.',steps:['Total=14.','Retained=13.','Ratio=13/14≈0.9286.'],result:'About 92.86% of centered squared variation retained.'}],
'For n>1, sample covariance is XᵀX/(n−1). Its eigenvalues equal σ²/(n−1), and its eigenvectors are right singular vectors. Truncated SVD gives a best rank-k approximation under Frobenius error. Feature scaling changes PCA: high-unit-variance features can dominate. Fit scaling and PCA on training data only, then reuse those transformations on validation and test data.',
'PCA is unsupervised. A low-variance direction can still contain the class signal. Never choose components by looking at held-out test performance repeatedly.',
'Compression, denoising, visualization and low-rank model approximations use SVD and PCA.',
'Singular values are 4 and 3. What variation fraction does the first preserve?',
'Use squared values: 16/(16+9)=0.64, or 64%.',
'PCA rotates centered data into ranked directions and keeps a selected subset.','pca');
add('derivatives','03 · Calculus','Limits and derivatives: measuring change',
'A derivative measures how fast a function changes at a particular input. A straight line has one slope everywhere; a curve can have a different slope at each point. A limit describes the value an expression approaches as the input gets closer to a point. Derivatives use limits to shrink an average slope into a local slope.',
'f′(x) = limₕ→₀ [f(x+h)−f(x)]/h     d(xᵏ)/dx = kxᵏ⁻¹',
['f′: derivative of f','h: a small nonzero input change before taking the limit','lim: limiting value, not simply substitution','d/dx: differentiate with respect to x'],
['Pick two nearby inputs x and x+h.','Divide their output difference by h to obtain the secant slope.','Let h approach zero, if the limit exists. The result is the tangent slope.','For small changes, approximate f(x+h) with f(x)+f′(x)h. This is a local approximation, not a global identity.'],
[{q:'Derive the derivative of x².',steps:['[(x+h)²−x²]/h = [2xh+h²]/h.','For nonzero h this equals 2x+h.','As h approaches 0, the result approaches 2x.'],result:'f′(x)=2x; at x=3 the slope is 6.'},{q:'Approximate 3.01² using the tangent at 3.',steps:['f(3)=9; f′(3)=6; h=0.01.','Approximation=9+6×0.01=9.06.','Exact value=9.0601.'],result:'Approximation error: 0.0001.'}],
'Differentiability implies continuity, but continuity alone does not imply differentiability. The absolute-value curve is continuous at zero but has left slope −1 and right slope 1. Numerical finite differences approximate derivatives but can suffer rounding and cancellation for very small h. Automatic differentiation applies derivative rules to a computation graph instead.',
'A derivative of zero does not always mean a minimum: x³ has zero derivative at zero but keeps increasing through that point.',
'Training needs derivatives to estimate which parameter changes will reduce the loss.',
'Differentiate 3x²+2x+5 and evaluate at x=2.',
'Derivative=6x+2. At x=2 it equals 14. The constant contributes zero.',
'A derivative is the slope of a function at one point.','curve');
add('chain','03 · Calculus','Product rule and chain rule',
'Models connect many functions. The chain rule tells us how a change travels through this chain. Multiply the sensitivity of each stage along a path. The product rule handles two changing quantities multiplied together. Keeping these rules separate prevents many derivative mistakes.',
'd(uv)/dx = u′v+uv′     d[g(f(x))]/dx = g′(f(x))f′(x)',
['u,v: functions of x','g′(f(x)): outer derivative evaluated at the inner output','f′(x): sensitivity of the inner function'],
['Name intermediate results so that each operation is simple.','Differentiate the outer operation while treating its input as a variable.','Multiply by the derivative of the inner operation.','If a variable influences the output along multiple paths, add the contributions from those paths.'],
[{q:'Differentiate (3x+1)² at x=2.',steps:['Let u=3x+1; then y=u².','dy/du=2u and du/dx=3.','dy/dx=6(3x+1). At x=2, u=7.'],result:'Derivative at 2: 42.'},{q:'Differentiate x·eˣ at x=0.',steps:['Product rule gives 1·eˣ+x·eˣ.','At x=0 this is 1+0.'],result:'Derivative: eˣ(1+x); value at zero: 1.'}],
'In a computation graph, reverse-mode differentiation starts from the scalar loss and propagates sensitivities backward. A reused intermediate node receives the sum of gradient contributions from all outgoing paths. This avoids recomputing entire derivatives for each parameter. Forward-mode differentiation instead propagates input perturbations toward outputs; the best mode depends on input and output dimensions.',
'Do not forget the inner derivative. Differentiating (3x+1)² as 2(3x+1) misses a factor of 3.',
'Backpropagation in neural networks is an efficient application of the multivariable chain rule.',
'Find d ln(2x+1)/dx for x>−1/2.',
'The outer derivative is 1/(2x+1); multiply by the inner derivative 2. Result: 2/(2x+1).',
'Differentiate one small operation at a time, multiply along paths and add across paths.','flow');
add('gradients','03 · Calculus','Partial derivatives, gradients and Jacobians',
'A loss usually depends on many parameters. A partial derivative changes one input while holding the others fixed. The gradient collects these partial derivatives into a vector. A Jacobian collects derivatives for a vector-valued output: one row per output, one column per input.',
'∇f = [∂f/∂x₁,…,∂f/∂xᵈ]ᵀ     Jᵢⱼ = ∂fᵢ/∂xⱼ     df ≈ ∇fᵀdx',
['∂: partial derivative','∇: gradient operator','J: Jacobian matrix','dx: a small vector change in the input'],
['Identify which variables are independent inputs.','For each partial derivative, treat all other independent inputs as constants.','Assemble the gradient in the same coordinate order as the input.','Its dot product with a unit direction gives the directional derivative. The negative gradient is locally steepest descent under Euclidean distance.'],
[{q:'For f(x,y)=x²+3y², find the gradient at (2,1).',steps:['∂f/∂x=2x.','∂f/∂y=6y.','Substitute x=2,y=1.'],result:'Gradient=[4,6].'},{q:'For F(x,y)=[x+y,xy], find J at (2,3).',steps:['First output has derivatives [1,1].','Second output has derivatives [y,x].'],result:'J=[[1,1],[3,2]].'}],
'The gradient direction depends on the coordinate scale and the metric used to define distance. Rescaling features can therefore alter optimization behavior. A large Jacobian norm can amplify small input changes; this connects sensitivity to robustness. Gradients in a batch loss may be sums or averages, which changes their numerical scale and interacts with the learning rate.',
'Holding y fixed is valid only if y is an independent input. If y depends on x, a total derivative must also include that path.',
'Gradients update parameters; Jacobians describe transformations and sensitivity in vector-valued networks.',
'For f(x,y)=xy+y², find ∇f at (3,2).',
'∂f/∂x=y=2; ∂f/∂y=x+2y=7. Gradient=[2,7].',
'A gradient tells how one output changes; a Jacobian handles many outputs.','curve');
add('curvature','03 · Calculus','Hessians and Taylor approximations',
'A first derivative measures slope. A second derivative measures how the slope changes: curvature. The Hessian is the matrix of second partial derivatives. A Taylor approximation uses local value, slope and curvature to build a nearby approximation of a function.',
'f(x+δ) ≈ f(x)+∇f(x)ᵀδ+½δᵀHδ     Hᵢⱼ = ∂²f/(∂xᵢ∂xⱼ)',
['δ (delta): a small displacement','H: Hessian matrix','½δᵀHδ: second-order curvature correction'],
['Find the gradient, then differentiate its entries again.','At a stationary point the gradient is zero, so curvature becomes especially informative.','A positive definite Hessian at a stationary point gives a strict local minimum; a negative definite Hessian gives a strict local maximum.','Mixed positive and negative eigenvalues give a saddle. Semidefinite or zero curvature can make the test inconclusive.'],
[{q:'Use a second-order expansion of x² at x=3 for δ=0.1.',steps:['Value=9, slope=6, second derivative=2.','Approximation=9+6×0.1+½×2×0.1².'],result:'9.61, exact because the function is quadratic.'},{q:'Classify the origin of f(x,y)=x²−y².',steps:['Gradient=[2x,−2y] is zero at the origin.','Hessian=diag(2,−2).','Curvature has both signs.'],result:'A saddle point, not a minimum.'}],
'Taylor approximations are local and require appropriate smoothness. Higher-order remainder terms determine the error. In high-dimensional models, explicitly storing a full Hessian is expensive: d parameters require d² entries. Hessian-vector products can reveal curvature without constructing that entire matrix.',
'A zero Hessian does not prove a flat function: x⁴ has zero second derivative at zero but a strict minimum there.',
'Newton methods, uncertainty approximations and diagnosing optimization difficulty use curvature.',
'For f(x)=5x², what is the second derivative?',
'f′(x)=10x and f″(x)=10. The curve has constant positive curvature.',
'Slope suggests a direction; curvature describes how quickly that suggestion changes.','curve');
add('integrals','03 · Calculus','Integrals, densities and change of variables',
'An integral adds many tiny contributions. For a nonnegative curve, a definite integral measures the area under the curve over an interval. In probability, density is not itself probability: integrating density over a range gives the probability of landing in that range.',
'P(a≤X≤b)=∫ₐᵇ p(x)dx     E[X]=∫ x p(x)dx     pY(y)=pX(x)|dx/dy|',
['∫: integrate, a continuous accumulation','dx: the variable and infinitesimal width of integration','p(x): probability density','Last formula: one-dimensional invertible differentiable transformation y=g(x)'],
['Decide what is being accumulated and over which interval.','For a constant density, probability is rectangle height times interval width.','An antiderivative F with F′=f gives ∫ₐᵇf(x)dx=F(b)−F(a) under standard continuity conditions.','When changing coordinates, adjust density for the stretching or compression of intervals. In multiple dimensions this uses the absolute Jacobian determinant.'],
[{q:'X is uniform on [0,4]. Find P(1≤X≤3).',steps:['Density=1/4 throughout [0,4].','Interval length=2.','Area=2×1/4.'],result:'Probability=0.5.'},{q:'Let Y=2X for X uniform on [0,1].',steps:['Y ranges from 0 to 2.','x=y/2, so |dx/dy|=1/2.','Density of Y is 1×1/2 on [0,2].'],result:'Density=0.5; total area=1.'}],
'An expectation is a weighted integral; weights are the probability density. A point can have zero probability even where density is positive. Multidimensional integrals become difficult in high dimensions, motivating Monte Carlo sampling. For non-invertible transformations, contributions from every valid preimage must be included; a single inverse-derivative formula is not enough.',
'A density can exceed 1. It is the total integrated probability that must equal 1, not the maximum curve height.',
'Continuous probability, Bayesian inference, normalizing flows and expected losses all use integrals.',
'Integrate 2x from 0 to 3.',
'An antiderivative is x². Evaluate 3²−0²=9.',
'Integrals turn local density or rate into accumulated probability or quantity.','distribution');
add('probability','04 · Probability','Events, conditional probability and independence',
'Probability assigns numbers from 0 to 1 to uncertain events. A conditional probability updates the sample space after you learn that another event happened. Independence means learning one event does not change the probability of the other. It is an assumption to justify, not a default fact about a dataset.',
'P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B)     P(A∩B)=P(A|B)P(B)',
['P: probability','A|B: event A given that B occurred','P(B)>0 is required for this elementary conditional formula','Independent events satisfy P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B)'],
['Identify the event you want and the information already known.','For conditioning, restrict attention to outcomes satisfying the known event.','Calculate the fraction or probability mass of the desired event within that restricted set.','Use independence to factor a joint probability only when the assumption is justified.'],
[{q:'A fair die is known to show an even number. What is the probability it shows a number above 3?',steps:['Allowed outcomes are now {2,4,6}.','Favorable outcomes are {4,6}.'],result:'P(above 3 | even)=2/3.'},{q:'Two independent fair coin tosses: probability of two heads?',steps:['First heads probability=1/2.','Second heads remains 1/2 after learning the first.','Multiply: 1/2×1/2.'],result:'1/4.'}],
'Conditional independence means two variables become independent after a third is known. It does not necessarily imply unconditional independence. Observational conditioning also differs from an intervention: P(Y|X=x) need not equal the outcome of deliberately setting X to x. Hidden common causes can create an association without a direct causal effect.',
'P(A|B) is usually not P(B|A). Disjoint events with positive probability cannot be independent, because observing one rules out the other.',
'Classification models estimate conditional label probabilities; probabilistic models factor joint distributions.',
'If P(A∩B)=0.12 and P(B)=0.3, find P(A|B).',
'0.12/0.3=0.4.',
'Conditional probability changes what you know; independence says that change does not affect the event of interest.','sets');
add('bayes','04 · Probability','Bayes’ rule and Bayesian updating',
'Bayes’ rule reverses a conditional probability. Start with a prior belief, measure how compatible the observed evidence is with each hypothesis, then normalize to obtain a posterior. A rare hypothesis can remain unlikely even after evidence that often appears when the hypothesis is true.',
'P(H|E) = P(E|H)P(H)/P(E)',
['H: hypothesis; E: observed evidence','P(H): prior probability','P(E|H): likelihood of the evidence under H','P(H|E): posterior probability','P(E): evidence probability, summed over hypotheses'],
['Write the prior for every possible hypothesis.','Multiply each prior by the likelihood of the evidence under that hypothesis.','Add these weighted likelihoods to obtain the evidence probability.','Divide each weighted likelihood by that total so posterior probabilities sum to 1.'],
[{q:'A fictional factory produces 10% faulty items. A flag appears for 80% of faulty and 20% of good items. What fraction of flagged items are faulty?',steps:['Faulty-and-flagged mass=0.1×0.8=0.08.','Good-and-flagged mass=0.9×0.2=0.18.','All flagged mass=0.26; posterior=0.08/0.26.'],result:'About 30.77%, not 80%.'},{q:'Two equally likely machines produce a marked item with probabilities 0.6 and 0.2. An item is marked.',steps:['Weighted masses=0.5×0.6=0.3 and 0.5×0.2=0.1.','Normalize the first by total 0.4.'],result:'Posterior probability of first machine=0.75.'}],
'In continuous parameter models, posterior density is proportional to likelihood times prior; normalization requires an integral. Conjugate priors allow analytic updates for some likelihood families. A prior expresses modeling assumptions, not certainty. Posterior predictive distributions average predictions over parameter uncertainty rather than using only one fitted parameter.',
'Do not ignore the base rate. A likelihood is a function of a hypothesis or parameter with observed data fixed; it need not sum to 1 over hypotheses.',
'Bayesian classifiers, uncertainty estimation and probabilistic model comparison use Bayes’ rule.',
'Prior odds are 1:3 and the evidence likelihood ratio is 6. What are posterior odds?',
'Multiply odds by the likelihood ratio: (1/3)×6=2. Posterior odds 2:1, or probability 2/3.',
'Posterior belief is prior belief reweighted by evidence.','bars');
add('moments','04 · Probability','Random variables, expectation and covariance',
'A random variable assigns a number to an uncertain outcome. Its expectation is a probability-weighted average, not a promise about a single result. Variance measures average squared distance from that mean. Covariance asks whether two variables tend to move above and below their means together.',
'E[X]=Σₓxp(x)     Var(X)=E[X²]−E[X]²     Cov(X,Y)=E[(X−μX)(Y−μY)]',
['μ (mu): a mean','Var: variance; standard deviation is √Var','Cov: covariance','Correlation ρ=Cov(X,Y)/(σXσY) when both deviations are nonzero'],
['Multiply each possible value by its probability and add to find the mean.','Subtract the mean, square the deviations and average with the same probabilities to find variance.','For covariance, multiply paired centered values before averaging.','Normalize covariance by both standard deviations to obtain a unit-free correlation.'],
[{q:'X is 0 or 2, each with probability 1/2.',steps:['Mean=0×0.5+2×0.5=1.','Squared deviations from 1 are both 1.'],result:'Mean=1; variance=1; standard deviation=1.'},{q:'Let Y=3X+2 for the same X.',steps:['E[Y]=3E[X]+2=5.','Var(Y)=3²Var(X)=9.','The added constant shifts the mean but not spread.'],result:'Mean=5; variance=9.'}],
'Expectation is linear even without independence: E[X+Y]=E[X]+E[Y]. Variance adds directly only when covariance is zero; generally Var(X+Y)=Var(X)+Var(Y)+2Cov(X,Y). Independence implies zero covariance when moments exist, but zero covariance does not imply independence. For symmetric X, X and X² can be dependent yet uncorrelated.',
'Do not average standard deviations to get a combined standard deviation. Variance is measured in squared units.',
'Expected risk, feature scaling, covariance matrices and portfolio-style uncertainty calculations use moments.',
'If Var(X)=4, what is Var(−2X+7)?',
'(−2)²×4=16. The constant 7 does not change variance.',
'Mean measures center; variance measures spread; covariance measures joint linear movement.','distribution');
add('distributions','04 · Probability','Bernoulli, binomial, Gaussian and other distributions',
'A distribution describes how probability is spread over possible values. Choose a family that fits the type of quantity: a binary outcome, a count, a category or a continuous measurement. A distribution is a modeling choice; real data may depart from it.',
'Bernoulli: P(X=x)=pˣ(1−p)¹⁻ˣ     Binomial: P(K=k)=C(n,k)pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ',
['p: success probability','n: number of independent trials with the same p','k: number of successes','Gaussian N(μ,σ²): continuous bell-shaped density with mean μ and variance σ²'],
['Use Bernoulli for one yes/no event and binomial for the sum of n independent equal-probability Bernoulli events.','Use categorical for one of several labels; category probabilities must sum to 1.','A Poisson distribution models certain counts with mean λ and variance λ; its assumptions need checking.','A Gaussian models a continuous quantity with symmetric spread. Standardize with z=(x−μ)/σ when σ>0.'],
[{q:'Three independent fair tosses: probability of exactly two heads?',steps:['Choose which two tosses are heads: C(3,2)=3.','Each ordered outcome has probability (1/2)³=1/8.'],result:'3/8=0.375.'},{q:'A Gaussian measurement has mean 10 and standard deviation 2. Standardize 13.',steps:['Distance from mean=3.','Divide by standard deviation 2.'],result:'z=1.5, meaning 1.5 standard deviations above the mean.'}],
'The exponential distribution models a nonnegative waiting time under a constant event rate; the beta distribution models a probability on [0,1]; the Dirichlet models a vector of category probabilities. Gaussian density is exp(−(x−μ)²/(2σ²))/(σ√(2π)). A mixture of Gaussians can model several clusters. Matching mean and variance alone does not identify an arbitrary distribution.',
'A Gaussian is unbounded and may be inappropriate for strictly positive values without a transformation. Overdispersed counts can violate the Poisson mean-equals-variance assumption.',
'Likelihood functions in classification, regression, count prediction and Bayesian models come from distribution families.',
'A Bernoulli variable has p=0.3. Find its mean and variance.',
'Mean=p=0.3. Variance=p(1−p)=0.3×0.7=0.21.',
'Choose a distribution based on the variable and assumptions, not just because its formula is familiar.','distribution');
add('sampling','04 · Probability','Sampling, the law of large numbers and Monte Carlo',
'A sample is a finite set of observations from a process. The law of large numbers explains why sample averages approach the population mean under suitable assumptions. Monte Carlo estimates an expectation by drawing samples and averaging function values. The central limit theorem explains the approximate shape of many sample-average errors.',
'μ̂ = (1/n)Σᵢ f(Xᵢ)     SE(average) ≈ σ/√n',
['μ̂: estimate of the desired expectation','Xᵢ: sampled values','SE: standard error, the spread of an estimator across repeated samples','σ: standard deviation of the sampled quantity'],
['Specify the distribution to sample from and the function whose expectation you need.','Draw independent representative samples when using the basic formulas.','Calculate the function value for each sample and average.','Quantify Monte Carlo error. With finite variance and independent samples, doubling accuracy typically requires about four times as many samples.'],
[{q:'Estimate E[X²] from sample values 0,1,2,3.',steps:['Squared values=0,1,4,9.','Average=(0+1+4+9)/4.'],result:'Monte Carlo estimate=3.5; not guaranteed to equal the population value.'},{q:'Population standard deviation is 10. Compare mean SE for n=100 and n=400.',steps:['At n=100: 10/√100=1.','At n=400: 10/√400=0.5.'],result:'Four times the sample size halves standard error.'}],
'The central limit theorem does not say the original data becomes Gaussian. It concerns a suitably normalized sum or mean as sample size increases under conditions such as independent identically distributed samples with finite variance. Correlated samples reduce effective sample size. Importance sampling changes the sampling distribution and reweights observations; mismatched weights can have enormous variance.',
'More biased samples do not remove selection bias. The law of large numbers does not require a coin to compensate for a recent streak.',
'Stochastic training, approximate Bayesian inference, simulation and evaluation uncertainty use sampling.',
'To reduce independent-sample standard error from 0.2 to 0.1, how should n change?',
'Multiply n by 4 because standard error scales like 1/√n.',
'Averages can estimate expectations, but representative sampling and uncertainty matter.','bars');
add('estimation','05 · Statistics','Estimation, maximum likelihood and MAP',
'An estimator is a rule for turning observed data into a parameter guess. Maximum likelihood estimation chooses parameters that make the observed data most probable under the model. Maximum a posteriori estimation also includes a prior. Neither procedure proves that the chosen model family matches reality.',
'θMLE = argmaxθ Σᵢ ln p(xᵢ|θ)     θMAP = argmaxθ [ln p(data|θ)+ln p(θ)]',
['θ (theta): unknown parameter or parameter vector','argmax: the argument that makes an expression largest','MLE: maximum likelihood estimate','MAP: maximum a posteriori estimate'],
['Select a probability model and state independence assumptions.','Write the joint likelihood of the observed data.','Take its logarithm to simplify products.','Differentiate, solve for stationary points and check boundaries or use numerical optimization. Add a log prior for MAP.'],
[{q:'Observe 3 heads and 1 tail. Estimate a Bernoulli head probability.',steps:['Likelihood=p³(1−p).','Log-likelihood=3ln p+ln(1−p).','Derivative=3/p−1/(1−p)=0 gives 3(1−p)=p.'],result:'MLE p=3/4=0.75.'},{q:'Use a Beta(2,2) prior for the same data.',steps:['Posterior is Beta(5,3).','The posterior mode is (5−1)/(5+3−2)=4/6.','The posterior mean is instead 5/8.'],result:'MAP=2/3; posterior mean=0.625. They are different summaries.'}],
'For Gaussian noise with fixed variance, maximizing likelihood is equivalent to minimizing squared error. A zero-mean Gaussian prior on weights adds an L2 penalty to negative log-likelihood, with a coefficient determined by the variance and loss scaling. MAP is a point estimate; a full Bayesian posterior retains uncertainty. Boundary maxima require care when derivative equations have no interior solution.',
'Likelihood is not the probability of the parameter. MLE uses data and a sampling model; a posterior also requires a prior.',
'Regression losses and regularization often have probabilistic interpretations through MLE and MAP.',
'For 8 successes in 10 independent Bernoulli trials, what is the MLE?',
'8/10=0.8.',
'Estimation turns data into parameter guesses; MLE and MAP differ in whether a prior is included.','bars');
add('inference','05 · Statistics','Confidence intervals and hypothesis tests',
'An estimate changes when the sample changes. Statistical inference describes that uncertainty under explicit assumptions. A confidence interval comes from a procedure with a repeated-sampling coverage rate. A hypothesis test asks whether the observed statistic is unusually extreme under a specified null model.',
'Approximate mean interval: x̄ ± 1.96·SE     z = (x̄−μ₀)/SE',
['x̄: sample mean','μ₀: mean claimed by the null model','SE: standard error','1.96: normal critical value for a two-sided 95% interval'],
['Specify the sampling process, statistic and null before examining results.','Estimate standard error with a method appropriate to dependence and sample size.','Construct an interval or compare the statistic with its null distribution.','Report effect size and uncertainty as well as a p-value. Multiple comparisons and repeated looks require adjustments.'],
[{q:'A sample mean is 50 with standard error 2; use a normal approximation.',steps:['Margin=1.96×2=3.92.','Subtract and add the margin.'],result:'Approximate 95% confidence interval: [46.08,53.92].'},{q:'Test null mean 46 using the same estimate.',steps:['z=(50−46)/2=2.','A two-sided normal tail beyond ±2 is approximately 0.0455.'],result:'Approximate p-value=0.0455 under the stated assumptions.'}],
'In frequentist language, the fixed population parameter is not random; 95% refers to the long-run fraction of intervals from this procedure that cover it. A Bayesian credible interval is a different object conditioned on a model and prior. For a small normal sample with estimated variance, a t interval is appropriate rather than blindly using 1.96. Bootstrapping estimates sampling variability by resampling, but must respect clustering or temporal dependence.',
'A p-value is not the probability that the null is true. Failing to reject is not proof of no effect. Statistical significance is not necessarily practical importance.',
'Model comparisons, experiments and reporting evaluation uncertainty use statistical inference.',
'If the SE doubles while the confidence level stays fixed, what happens to the margin?',
'It doubles because the margin is the critical value multiplied by SE.',
'Always pair an estimate with the assumptions and uncertainty behind it.','distribution');
add('generalization','05 · Statistics','Bias, variance and honest evaluation',
'A model must work on new data, not merely remember training examples. Bias describes systematic error from restrictive assumptions. Variance describes how much a fitted predictor changes with the training sample. Irreducible noise is variation that the available features and target definition do not explain.',
'Expected squared prediction error = bias² + variance + noise variance',
['Bias: average prediction minus the true conditional mean','Variance: spread of predictions across repeated training datasets','Noise: residual target variation at fixed input','Decomposition assumes squared error and the usual zero-mean noise model'],
['Split data to reflect the intended future use: random, grouped or time-ordered as appropriate.','Fit transformations and model parameters on training data only.','Use validation data or cross-validation for model choices. Keep final test data for a final assessment.','Compare training and validation errors to diagnose underfitting or overfitting, while accounting for sampling uncertainty.'],
[{q:'At one input, the true mean is 5. Two equally likely fitted models predict 4 and 8; noise variance is 1.',steps:['Average prediction=6, so bias²=(6−5)²=1.','Prediction variance=[(4−6)²+(8−6)²]/2=4.','Add noise variance 1.'],result:'Expected squared prediction error=6.'},{q:'You standardize using the full dataset before cross-validation.',steps:['Validation means and variances influence training preprocessing.','Fit the scaler separately inside each training fold instead.'],result:'The full-data procedure leaks validation information.'}],
'Cross-validation estimates performance of a training procedure, not a universal property of an algorithm. Nested cross-validation separates hyperparameter selection from outer evaluation. Distribution shift can invalidate a random-split estimate even when no leakage occurs. Capacity, regularization, sample size and optimization all interact; simple bias-variance stories do not explain every modern model behavior.',
'Repeatedly choosing models based on the test set turns it into validation data. Accuracy on duplicated or related train/test records can be misleading.',
'Every ML workflow needs honest splitting, leakage control and an appropriate evaluation target.',
'Where should a feature mean be fitted before evaluating on the test set?',
'On the training data only. Apply that stored mean to the test data.',
'Generalization is about future examples; protect the evaluation process from information leakage.','flow');
add('metrics','05 · Statistics','Classification metrics and decision thresholds',
'A probability prediction and a yes/no decision are different. A threshold turns a score into a decision. Precision asks how many predicted positives are correct. Recall asks how many actual positives were found. The right balance depends on the consequence of each type of error.',
'Precision=TP/(TP+FP)     Recall=TP/(TP+FN)     F1=2PR/(P+R)',
['TP: true positives; FP: false positives','FN: false negatives; TN: true negatives','P and R in F1 mean precision and recall','Accuracy=(TP+TN)/(TP+FP+FN+TN)'],
['Create a confusion matrix from held-out predictions at a chosen threshold.','Compute metrics with denominators matching the question you care about.','Lowering a threshold usually increases both true positives and false positives; compare tradeoffs on validation data.','Evaluate calibration separately: among predictions near 0.7, approximately 70% should be positive if well calibrated in that group.'],
[{q:'TP=30, FP=10, FN=20, TN=40.',steps:['Precision=30/40=0.75.','Recall=30/50=0.6.','F1=2×0.75×0.6/(0.75+0.6)=2/3.'],result:'Precision 75%; recall 60%; F1≈0.6667; accuracy 70%.'},{q:'Only 1% of examples are positive. Predict negative for every example.',steps:['99% are classified correctly.','No positive examples are found.'],result:'Accuracy 99%; recall 0. High accuracy hides failure.'}],
'ROC curves compare true-positive and false-positive rates across thresholds. Precision–recall curves are often more informative for rare positives. Ranking quality, calibration and decision utility are distinct. With calibrated probabilities and only false-positive cost cFP and false-negative cost cFN, predicting positive minimizes expected cost when p>cFP/(cFP+cFN). Real decisions may impose additional constraints.',
'Precision is undefined when no positives are predicted; implementations choose a convention. Report it rather than silently treating all undefined cases as successes.',
'Spam filtering, anomaly detection and classification evaluation need metrics matched to the task.',
'With cFP=1 and cFN=4, what is the probability threshold under the stated simple cost model?',
'1/(1+4)=0.2.',
'Measure the errors that matter, and separate probability quality from threshold decisions.','bars');
add('entropy','06 · Information theory','Entropy, cross-entropy and KL divergence',
'Information theory quantifies uncertainty and prediction mismatch. Entropy is the average surprise of outcomes under their true distribution. Cross-entropy measures the average surprise when a predicted distribution assigns the probabilities. KL divergence is the extra cross-entropy caused by using the wrong distribution.',
'H(p)=−Σᵢpᵢln pᵢ     H(p,q)=−Σᵢpᵢln qᵢ     KL(p‖q)=H(p,q)−H(p)',
['p: target distribution; q: predicted distribution','H: entropy or cross-entropy as shown','KL: Kullback–Leibler divergence','Natural logarithms give nats; base-2 logarithms give bits'],
['Compute surprise as −log(probability). Rare outcomes have more surprise.','Average surprises using the actual outcome probabilities to obtain entropy.','Replace the probability inside the logarithm with model probability to obtain cross-entropy.','Subtract entropy to measure mismatch. KL is nonnegative when defined, but is generally asymmetric.'],
[{q:'A fair binary variable has probabilities [0.5,0.5]. Use base 2.',steps:['Each outcome has surprise −log₂(0.5)=1.','Weighted average=0.5×1+0.5×1.'],result:'Entropy=1 bit.'},{q:'Target is class 1 and model gives it probability 0.8.',steps:['One-hot target makes every other weighted term zero.','Cross-entropy=−ln(0.8).'],result:'Loss≈0.2231 nats.'}],
'Minimizing cross-entropy for fixed targets is equivalent to minimizing KL because H(p) is constant with respect to the model. If q assigns zero probability where p is positive, KL is infinite. The convention 0 log 0=0 is defined by a limit. Mutual information is KL between a joint distribution and the product of its marginals, measuring general dependence rather than only linear association.',
'KL is not a distance metric: it is asymmetric and does not satisfy the triangle inequality. Differential entropy for continuous variables can be negative.',
'Classification losses, variational inference, compression and information-based feature analysis use these quantities.',
'What is the entropy of a certain outcome with probability 1?',
'−1×log(1)=0. There is no uncertainty.',
'Entropy is uncertainty; cross-entropy adds model mismatch; KL isolates that mismatch.','bars');
add('numerics','06 · Information theory','Floating point and stable softmax',
'Computers represent real numbers using finite precision. Some calculations overflow, underflow or lose important digits when nearly equal numbers are subtracted. A mathematically correct formula can therefore be a poor computational recipe. Stable algorithms rewrite the same mathematics to keep intermediate values in a safe numerical range.',
'softmax(z)ᵢ = exp(zᵢ−m)/Σⱼexp(zⱼ−m)     m=maxⱼzⱼ',
['z: unnormalized class scores, called logits','m: largest score','softmax: positive outputs summing to 1','logsumexp(z)=m+ln Σⱼexp(zⱼ−m)'],
['Subtract the largest logit from every logit. This leaves softmax probabilities unchanged.','Exponentiate the shifted scores; none exceeds 1.','Sum and divide. For log probabilities, compute zᵢ−logsumexp(z) directly.','Avoid rounding intermediate calculations. Round only the displayed answer when teaching or reporting.'],
[{q:'Calculate softmax for [1000,1000].',steps:['Direct exp(1000) can overflow.','Subtract 1000 to get [0,0].','Exponentials=[1,1]; divide by sum 2.'],result:'[0.5,0.5] without overflow.'},{q:'Calculate softmax for [0,ln 3].',steps:['Unshifted exponentials would be [1,3].','Their sum is 4.','The stable shifted method produces the same ratios.'],result:'[0.25,0.75].'}],
'Machine epsilon describes spacing near 1, not a universal absolute error bound. A problem can be ill-conditioned even when the algorithm is stable: small input errors may inherently cause large output errors. Mixed-precision training saves memory but may need loss scaling and higher-precision accumulators. Subtracting the maximum prevents softmax overflow, though tiny output probabilities can still underflow; log-softmax avoids taking their logarithm afterward.',
'Do not compute log(softmax(z)) naively for extreme logits. Use a stable log-softmax implementation.',
'Reliable neural network training depends on numerical computation as well as symbolic mathematics.',
'What is softmax([5,5,5])?',
'All logits are equal, so each probability is 1/3.',
'Equivalent formulas can behave differently on a computer; keep intermediate values well scaled.','bars');
add('descent','07 · Optimization','Gradient descent and learning rate',
'Optimization searches for parameters that reduce an objective. Gradient descent repeatedly moves opposite the gradient. The learning rate controls the step size. A gradient describes local change, so a step that is too large can overshoot and increase loss.',
'θnext = θ − η∇L(θ)',
['θ: parameter vector','η (eta): positive learning rate','L: objective or loss','∇L: gradient evaluated at current parameters'],
['Choose initial parameters and calculate the current loss.','Calculate the gradient at those parameters.','Multiply it by the learning rate and subtract from the parameters.','Recalculate the loss and repeat. Monitor convergence, validation performance and numerical problems separately.'],
[{q:'Minimize L(w)=(w−3)² from w=0 with η=0.1.',steps:['Gradient=2(w−3)=−6.','Next w=0−0.1×(−6)=0.6.','Loss falls from 9 to (0.6−3)²=5.76.'],result:'First update: w=0.6, loss=5.76.'},{q:'Use η=1.2 instead from w=0.',steps:['Next w=0−1.2×(−6)=7.2.','Loss=(7.2−3)²=17.64.'],result:'Loss increased: the step was too large.'}],
'For this one-dimensional quadratic, the error obeys e_next=(1−2η)e. It converges exactly when |1−2η|<1, or 0<η<1. For a smooth convex function with gradient Lipschitz constant Ls, step-size guarantees depend on Ls; the same numerical learning rate is not appropriate for every problem. In nonconvex models, gradient descent does not guarantee finding the global minimum.',
'A decreasing training loss is not proof of improving generalization. A small gradient can indicate a minimum, saddle, plateau or poor scaling.',
'Most neural-network training methods are variations on gradient-based optimization.',
'For L(w)=w², w=2 and η=0.25, compute one update.',
'Gradient=4. New w=2−0.25×4=1. New loss=1 instead of 4.',
'Gradient descent makes repeated local improvements; learning rate determines how far each step goes.','curve');
add('stochastic','07 · Optimization','Mini-batches, momentum and Adam',
'A full gradient averages contributions from every training example. A mini-batch uses a subset, making updates cheaper but noisy. Momentum smooths updates using past gradients. Adam maintains moving estimates of both gradient and squared-gradient magnitude to scale updates per coordinate.',
'gB=(1/|B|)Σᵢ∈B∇ℓᵢ     vₜ=βvₜ₋₁+gₜ     θₜ₊₁=θₜ−ηvₜ',
['B: a sampled mini-batch','ℓᵢ: loss for example i','v: momentum accumulator in this stated convention','β: memory coefficient, usually between 0 and 1'],
['Sample a representative batch and average its gradients.','For momentum, combine the new gradient with a decayed previous accumulator.','For Adam, compute first moment m=β₁m+(1−β₁)g and second moment v=β₂v+(1−β₂)g² elementwise.','Correct initial bias with m̂=m/(1−β₁ᵗ), v̂=v/(1−β₂ᵗ), then update by ηm̂/(√v̂+ε).'],
[{q:'Batch gradients are 2 and 6; η=0.1 and current w=5.',steps:['Average gradient=(2+6)/2=4.','Update=5−0.1×4.'],result:'w becomes 4.6.'},{q:'First Adam step: g=2, β₁=0.9, β₂=0.999, η=0.1, initial moments zero.',steps:['m=0.2 and v=0.004.','Bias correction gives m̂=2 and v̂=4.','Ignoring tiny ε, update magnitude=0.1×2/2.'],result:'Parameter decreases by about 0.1.'}],
'An unbiased mini-batch gradient requires suitable sampling and loss averaging. Momentum conventions differ: some use (1−β)g, so compare equations rather than names. Adam is not guaranteed to beat SGD on every task. Decoupled weight decay, as in AdamW, is not generally equivalent to adding an L2 term to the loss under adaptive scaling.',
'Changing batch size or changing a loss from mean to sum changes gradient scale. Preserve the intended learning-rate interpretation.',
'Large datasets need stochastic updates; momentum and adaptive scaling can improve practical training behavior.',
'Using v_next=0.9v+g with v=2 and g=−1, find v_next.',
'0.9×2−1=0.8.',
'Mini-batches trade exactness for speed; optimizer state controls how noisy gradients become updates.','flow');
add('convexity','07 · Optimization','Convexity, constraints and regularization',
'A convex function lies below the straight chord connecting any two points on its graph. On a convex domain, every local minimum of a convex function is global. Constraints limit acceptable solutions. Regularization adds a preference, such as smaller weights, to discourage unstable or overly complex fits.',
'J(w)=Ldata(w)+λ‖w‖₂²     Lagrangian=L(w)+αg(w)',
['λ≥0: regularization strength','L2 penalty: sum of squared weights','L1 penalty: sum of absolute weights','g(w)=0: an equality constraint; α: its multiplier'],
['State the objective and feasible set separately.','Check whether the objective and constraint set are convex before applying convex guarantees.','For L2 regularization, add 2λw to the gradient under the displayed penalty convention.','For equality constraints, introduce multipliers and solve stationarity plus feasibility. Inequality constraints additionally require sign and complementary-slackness conditions.'],
[{q:'Minimize (w−4)²+λw² with λ=1.',steps:['Derivative=2(w−4)+2w=4w−8.','Set to zero: w=2.','Without the penalty, the optimum would be 4.'],result:'Regularization shrinks the optimum to 2.'},{q:'Minimize x²+y² subject to x+y=1.',steps:['Lagrangian=x²+y²+α(x+y−1).','Stationarity gives 2x+α=0 and 2y+α=0, hence x=y.','The constraint then gives 2x=1.'],result:'x=y=0.5; objective=0.5.'}],
'Strict convexity can imply uniqueness, but ordinary convexity permits multiple minimizers. L1 penalties are not differentiable at zero and can produce sparse solutions; subgradient or proximal methods handle this. For inequality-constrained convex problems, suitable constraint qualifications are needed for strong duality and KKT guarantees. Neural networks generally produce nonconvex objectives, so convex theory does not transfer unchanged.',
'Regularization strength depends on feature scaling and whether data loss is summed or averaged. Choose it using validation data.',
'Ridge, lasso, support vector machines and constrained learning use these principles.',
'For penalty λw² with λ=0.5 and w=3, what gradient contribution does it add?',
'2λw=2×0.5×3=3.',
'Constraints define what is allowed; regularization defines what is preferred.','curve');
add('regression','08 · Mathematics inside models','Linear regression from prediction to training',
'Linear regression predicts a numeric target using a weighted sum of features. Learning adjusts weights to reduce squared residuals. The model is linear in its parameters even when the features include transformations such as x². A good fit still requires suitable data, evaluation and assumptions.',
'ŷ=Xw     L=(1/n)‖Xw−y‖₂²     ∇wL=(2/n)Xᵀ(Xw−y)',
['X: design matrix, one row per example','A column of ones can represent an intercept','w: weights; y: target vector','n: number of examples'],
['Build the design matrix, including an intercept if desired.','Multiply X by w to get all predictions.','Subtract targets, square residuals and average.','Use the gradient or a stable least-squares solver. Evaluate on examples excluded from fitting.'],
[{q:'For x=[1,2], y=[2,4], fit ŷ=wx starting at w=1.',steps:['Predictions=[1,2]; residuals=[−1,−2].','MSE=(1+4)/2=2.5.','Gradient=(2/2)[1×(−1)+2×(−2)]=−5.','With η=0.1, new w=1.5.'],result:'New predictions=[1.5,3]; new MSE=0.625.'},{q:'Find the exact no-intercept solution for the same data.',steps:['w=Σxy/Σx².','Numerator=1×2+2×4=10.','Denominator=1²+2²=5.'],result:'w=2, with zero training residual.'}],
'With independent Gaussian residuals of constant variance, least squares is maximum likelihood for the mean parameters. Heteroscedastic residuals may motivate weighted least squares or other models. Polynomial regression adds columns such as x² and x³; it stays linear in weights but can extrapolate badly. A zero training residual with very few observations is weak evidence of future accuracy.',
'Do not infer causation from a coefficient without a justified causal design. Correlated features can make coefficients unstable.',
'This is the simplest complete example linking matrices, derivatives, probability and optimization.',
'For ŷ=2x+1 and x=4, actual y=10, find the residual and squared residual.',
'Prediction=9; residual=−1; squared residual=1.',
'Regression turns feature vectors into numbers and learns by reducing prediction residuals.','line');
add('logistic','08 · Mathematics inside models','Logistic regression and classification loss',
'Logistic regression converts a linear score into a binary probability using a sigmoid. Its name contains regression, but its usual task is classification. The sigmoid keeps the output between 0 and 1; cross-entropy gives a loss consistent with a Bernoulli observation model.',
'z=wᵀx+b     p=1/(1+e⁻ᶻ)     ℓ=−yln p−(1−y)ln(1−p)',
['z: real-valued score or logit','p: predicted probability of class 1','y: observed binary label, either 0 or 1','∂ℓ/∂z=p−y'],
['Compute the linear score from features and weights.','Apply sigmoid to obtain a probability.','Use the observed class probability in the negative logarithm loss.','The chain rule simplifies the score derivative to p−y. Multiply by features for weight gradients.'],
[{q:'Score z=0 and target y=1.',steps:['Sigmoid(0)=1/2.','Loss=−ln(0.5)≈0.6931.','Score derivative=0.5−1=−0.5.'],result:'Increasing the score locally reduces this example’s loss.'},{q:'Score z=ln 3 and target y=0.',steps:['p=1/(1+1/3)=0.75.','Loss=−ln(0.25)≈1.3863.','Score derivative=0.75−0=0.75.'],result:'The model is too positive; training pushes this score down.'}],
'The log-odds ln[p/(1−p)] equals the linear score. A unit feature change multiplies odds by exp(weight), holding other features fixed. Logistic loss is convex in ordinary linear-model parameters, but not when the score is produced by a nonlinear neural network. With perfectly separable data, unregularized maximum-likelihood weights can grow without a finite optimum.',
'A score of 0 is probability 0.5, not probability 0. A decision threshold is chosen separately from training the probability model.',
'Binary classification and the output layer of many neural networks use this mathematics.',
'If p=0.2 and y=1, what is ∂ℓ/∂z?',
'p−y=−0.8.',
'Sigmoid makes a probability; cross-entropy teaches the score which way to move.','curve');
add('backprop','08 · Mathematics inside models','Neurons, activations and backpropagation',
'A neuron computes a weighted sum and passes it through an activation function. Several layers create a composition of functions. Backpropagation computes how every parameter contributed to a scalar loss by applying the chain rule backward through this composition.',
'z=wx+b     a=ReLU(z)=max(0,z)     L=½(a−y)²',
['z: pre-activation value','a: activation or neuron output','ReLU: rectified linear unit','Half-squared loss removes a factor of 2 from its derivative'],
['Forward pass: calculate intermediate values and the final loss.','Start backward with ∂L/∂a=a−y.','Multiply by the activation derivative: ReLU has derivative 1 for z>0 and 0 for z<0.','Multiply by x for the weight gradient and by 1 for the bias gradient. Update only after the gradients for the current parameters are computed.'],
[{q:'Use x=2,w=1,b=0,y=3.',steps:['z=2; a=2; loss=½(2−3)²=0.5.','∂L/∂a=−1; activation derivative=1.','∂L/∂w=−1×1×2=−2; ∂L/∂b=−1.','At η=0.1, w becomes 1.2 and b becomes 0.1.'],result:'New output=2.5; new loss=0.125.'},{q:'Use z=−2 for a ReLU neuron.',steps:['Output is 0.','Local activation derivative is 0.','Any incoming gradient is multiplied by 0 at this node.'],result:'No gradient passes through this negative ReLU on this example.'}],
'Repeated products of small derivatives can cause vanishing gradients; products with large magnitudes can explode. Initialization, residual connections and normalization help control signal and gradient scales. ReLU is nondifferentiable exactly at zero; implementations choose a convention, commonly zero. Backpropagation computes derivatives, while an optimizer decides how to use them. These are separate algorithms.',
'Do not change a weight midway through a backward pass and then use the changed weight to calculate gradients that should refer to the old computation.',
'Deep networks combine linear algebra, nonlinear functions and efficient chain-rule evaluation.',
'For a linear neuron a=wx, half-squared loss, x=3,w=2,y=4, find dL/dw.',
'a=6; dL/da=2; da/dw=3; gradient=6.',
'Forward computes a prediction; backward measures responsibility; the optimizer changes parameters.','network');
add('convolution','08 · Mathematics inside models','Convolution, pooling and Fourier intuition',
'A convolution-style layer applies a small shared filter across positions. Sharing weights lets the same pattern detector operate throughout an image or sequence. Deep-learning libraries usually implement cross-correlation without flipping the filter, though the layer is commonly called convolution.',
'yᵢ=Σₖwₖxᵢ₊ₖ     output size=floor((N+2P−K)/S)+1',
['K: filter width; N: input width','P: zero padding on each side; S: stride','Formula assumes dilation 1','w: shared filter coefficients'],
['Choose a filter, padding and stride.','At each valid location, multiply aligned input and filter entries and sum.','Slide by the stride and repeat using the same coefficients.','Pooling summarizes local neighborhoods, such as taking their maximum; it has different behavior from a learned filter.'],
[{q:'Cross-correlate [1,2,3,4] with filter [1,−1], stride 1 and no padding.',steps:['First window: 1−2=−1.','Second: 2−3=−1.','Third: 3−4=−1.'],result:'Output=[−1,−1,−1], a local difference detector.'},{q:'Input width 7, filter width 3, padding 1, stride 2.',steps:['Compute floor((7+2−3)/2)+1.','This equals floor(6/2)+1.'],result:'Output width=4.'}],
'The Fourier transform represents a signal as weighted frequencies. Under appropriate boundary conventions, convolution in space corresponds to multiplication in frequency. Discrete circular convolution differs from linear convolution; zero-padding is needed for an FFT computation of linear convolution without wraparound. Translation equivariance means shifting the input shifts the output; padding, stride and boundaries can limit exact equivariance.',
'Do not assume a convolution automatically makes a network invariant to translations. Equivariance and invariance are different properties.',
'Image models, audio processing and signal filters combine local structure, shared weights and frequency analysis.',
'Max-pool [1,5,2,4] with window 2 and stride 2.',
'Maxima of [1,5] and [2,4] are 5 and 4. Output=[5,4].',
'Convolution reuses one local rule at many locations; Fourier analysis views signals through frequency components.','matrix');
add('attention','08 · Mathematics inside models','Attention and the mathematics of transformers',
'Attention lets one token combine information from other tokens using data-dependent weights. A query asks what information is relevant. Keys describe what each token offers. Values carry the information to combine. A softmax turns query-key compatibility scores into a weighted average.',
'Attention(Q,K,V)=softmax(QKᵀ/√dₖ + mask)V',
['Q: query matrix; K: key matrix; V: value matrix','dₖ: dimension of each query and key','softmax is applied separately across keys for each query','mask: 0 for allowed positions, effectively −∞ for blocked positions'],
['Project token embeddings into queries, keys and values.','Compute dot products between every query and key.','Scale, mask forbidden positions, then normalize each row with softmax.','Multiply normalized weights by values to produce a weighted combination for each query.'],
[{q:'A query has scores [0,ln 3] and scalar values [2,6].',steps:['Softmax weights=[1/4,3/4].','Weighted value=(1/4)×2+(3/4)×6.'],result:'Attention output=5.'},{q:'There are 4 tokens, query/key dimension 3 and value dimension 2.',steps:['Q and K have shape (4,3).','QKᵀ has shape (4,4).','Multiplying weights by V of shape (4,2) gives (4,2).'],result:'Each token receives a 2-coordinate output.'}],
'If independent query/key coordinates have roughly unit variance, their dot-product variance grows with dₖ; division by √dₖ helps control score scale. Multiple heads learn different projections. Positional information is needed because ordinary attention alone does not identify token order. Transformers also use feedforward blocks, normalization and residual connections. A causal mask prevents a position from seeing future tokens during autoregressive training.',
'Attention weights are not automatically a faithful explanation of a model’s decision. Without masking, a language-model training example could leak future-token information.',
'Transformers combine matrix multiplication, softmax, weighted sums and gradient-based learning.',
'Equal attention scores over values [3,9] give what output?',
'Weights are [0.5,0.5], so output=6.',
'Attention builds a context-dependent weighted sum of information.','network');
add('kernels','09 · Advanced connections','Distances, kernels and support vector machines',
'A distance measures separation. A kernel measures an inner product in a possibly implicit feature space. Kernel methods can learn nonlinear boundaries without explicitly constructing every transformed feature. A support vector machine chooses a separating boundary with a large geometric margin, allowing controlled violations when needed.',
'k(x,z)=φ(x)ᵀφ(z)     RBF k(x,z)=exp(−‖x−z‖²/(2σ²))',
['φ: feature mapping','σ>0: RBF length scale','Margin distance: |wᵀx+b|/‖w‖ for a nonzero normal vector w','PSD Gram matrix: kernel values for all pairs must form a positive semidefinite matrix'],
['Choose a representation and scale features so the intended notion of similarity is meaningful.','Evaluate pairwise kernels instead of explicit high-dimensional feature dot products.','An SVM balances margin size against hinge-loss penalties max(0,1−y(wᵀx+b)), with labels −1 or +1.','Only some training points become support vectors, influencing the boundary in the dual representation.'],
[{q:'Compute k(x,z)=(xz+1)² for scalar x=2,z=3.',steps:['xz+1=7.','Square it: 49.','An explicit map is φ(x)=[x²,√2x,1], whose dot product gives the same polynomial.'],result:'Kernel value=49.'},{q:'RBF with distance 2 and σ=1.',steps:['Squared distance=4.','Exponent=−4/2=−2.'],result:'Similarity=e⁻²≈0.1353.'}],
'A symmetric similarity function is not automatically a valid kernel; all finite Gram matrices must be PSD for the standard positive-semidefinite kernel theory. The kernel trick can save feature construction but storing pairwise similarities can require quadratic memory in the number of examples. Distance concentration in high dimensions makes representation choice especially important.',
'A larger RBF length scale makes similarity decay more slowly, not more quickly. Scaling features changes kernel values.',
'SVMs, Gaussian processes and kernel PCA connect geometry with nonlinear learning.',
'For y=1 and score 0.3, calculate hinge loss.',
'max(0,1−0.3)=0.7.',
'A kernel lets an algorithm use feature-space geometry without always constructing those features.','vector');
add('clustering','09 · Advanced connections','K-means, Gaussian mixtures and EM',
'Clustering groups examples without supplied class labels. K-means assigns each point to its closest center and then moves each center to its assigned mean. Gaussian mixtures instead give probabilistic cluster membership. Expectation–maximization alternates between estimating hidden memberships and updating model parameters.',
'K-means objective=Σᵢ‖xᵢ−μcᵢ‖²     rᵢₖ=πₖN(xᵢ|μₖ,Σₖ)/ΣⱼπⱼN(xᵢ|μⱼ,Σⱼ)',
['cᵢ: assigned cluster for point i','μₖ: cluster center','πₖ: mixture weight, nonnegative and summing to 1','rᵢₖ: responsibility, the soft membership of point i in component k'],
['Initialize centers or mixture parameters.','For K-means, assign each point to its nearest center, then take means within groups.','For a Gaussian mixture E-step, compute normalized component responsibilities.','In the M-step, use responsibilities to compute weighted means, covariances and mixture weights. Repeat and monitor the objective.'],
[{q:'Points [0,2,8,10], initial centers 0 and 10.',steps:['0 and 2 join the first cluster; 8 and 10 the second.','New means=(0+2)/2=1 and (8+10)/2=9.','Squared error becomes 1+1+1+1.'],result:'Centers [1,9]; objective=4.'},{q:'Two weighted component densities for a point are 0.12 and 0.08.',steps:['Total density=0.20.','Normalize each contribution.'],result:'Responsibilities=[0.6,0.4].'}],
'K-means is appropriate for squared Euclidean geometry and tends toward compact clusters; initialization can lead to different local optima. Exact EM steps do not decrease observed-data likelihood, but do not guarantee a global optimum. Unconstrained Gaussian mixture likelihood can become singular when a component collapses onto a point, so covariance regularization and model selection matter.',
'A discovered cluster is not automatically a real-world category. Feature scale and chosen cluster count strongly influence the result.',
'Segmentation, density estimation and latent-variable learning use clustering and EM.',
'What is the new K-means center of [1,4,7]?',
'Their mean is (1+4+7)/3=4.',
'K-means uses hard nearest-center groups; mixture models use probabilistic memberships.','pca');
add('graphs','09 · Advanced connections','Graphs, Markov chains and message passing',
'A graph contains nodes and edges. It can represent relationships such as connections between fictional documents. An adjacency matrix records edges. Graph learning combines information along these relationships. A Markov chain instead describes transitions between states, assuming the next state depends on the present state under the model.',
'hᵢnew = mean{hⱼ : j is a neighbor of i}     πnext = πP',
['hᵢ: node feature vector','P: row-stochastic transition matrix; each row sums to 1','π: row vector of current state probabilities','Stationary distribution satisfies π=πP'],
['Define what nodes, edges and feature values mean.','Choose how neighbors contribute: sum, mean or learned attention.','Combine the aggregated information with a node’s own representation and a learned transformation.','For a Markov chain, multiply state probabilities by the transition matrix to move one step.'],
[{q:'Node A has neighbors with scalar features 2 and 8.',steps:['Sum neighboring features: 10.','Divide by 2 neighbors.'],result:'Mean-aggregated feature=5.'},{q:'P=[[0.8,0.2],[0.1,0.9]], initial π=[1,0].',steps:['After one step πP=[0.8,0.2].','After two: [0.8×0.8+0.2×0.1,0.8×0.2+0.2×0.9].'],result:'Two-step distribution=[0.66,0.34].'}],
'The graph Laplacian L=D−A combines degree and adjacency. For an undirected weighted graph, xᵀLx equals a sum of weighted squared differences across edges, encouraging neighboring values to be similar. Repeated averaging can oversmooth node representations. A finite irreducible Markov chain has a unique stationary distribution; aperiodicity is additionally needed for convergence to it from arbitrary initial distributions.',
'Do not assume every Markov chain converges: periodic chains may oscillate. A mean over an empty neighbor set needs an explicit convention.',
'Graph neural networks, ranking algorithms and state-based models use graph and transition mathematics.',
'For the displayed two-state P, solve its stationary distribution.',
'Let π=[a,1−a]. a=0.8a+0.1(1−a), so 0.3a=0.1 and π=[1/3,2/3].',
'Graphs describe relationships; message passing mixes neighbor information; Markov chains propagate probabilities.','network');
add('rl','09 · Advanced connections','Reinforcement learning and Bellman equations',
'Reinforcement learning studies actions that affect future rewards. A policy chooses actions from states. A value function estimates expected future return. Unlike ordinary supervised examples, an action can change which data will be observed next. Discounting gives more weight to near-term rewards and can make infinite returns finite.',
'Gₜ=Σₖ≥₀γᵏrₜ₊ₖ₊₁     Vπ(s)=Eπ[r+γVπ(s′)|s]',
['γ (gamma): discount factor, typically 0≤γ<1 for continuing tasks','π: policy','s′: next state','Vπ: expected return following policy π'],
['Define states, actions, rewards and transition assumptions.','Calculate return by discounting rewards according to how far in the future they arrive.','Use the Bellman decomposition: immediate reward plus discounted future value.','Estimate values from experience, then improve decisions; distinguish policy evaluation from finding an optimal policy.'],
[{q:'Rewards are 2 now, 4 next and then the episode ends; γ=0.5.',steps:['First reward contributes 2.','Next reward contributes 0.5×4=2.'],result:'Return=4.'},{q:'Current V(s)=3, reward=2, V(s′)=4, γ=0.5 and step size α=0.1.',steps:['Temporal-difference target=2+0.5×4=4.','Error=4−3=1.','Update V(s)=3+0.1×1.'],result:'Updated value=3.1.'}],
'For a fixed policy, the Bellman equation is an expectation equation. Optimal control replaces policy averaging over actions with maximization when appropriate. Under standard discounted finite-state assumptions the Bellman operator is a contraction, supporting convergence of dynamic programming. Function approximation, off-policy learning and bootstrapping together can destabilize learning; simple tabular guarantees do not automatically carry over.',
'Reward is not the same as value. At a terminal state there is no additional future return. A high immediate reward may lead to a poor long-term result.',
'Sequential decision systems connect probability, optimization and dynamic programming.',
'One continuing state gives reward 1 every step and γ=0.9. Find its value.',
'V=1+0.9V, so 0.1V=1 and V=10.',
'Bellman equations break a long future into one reward plus a smaller future-value problem.','flow');
add('generative','10 · Generative mathematics','Latent variables, autoencoders and variational inference',
'A latent variable represents hidden structure that is not directly observed. An autoencoder compresses data into a code and reconstructs it. A variational autoencoder adds a probabilistic latent model and an approximate posterior. Variational inference optimizes a simpler distribution to approximate a difficult posterior.',
'ELBO=Eq(z|x)[ln p(x|z)]−KL(q(z|x)‖p(z))     z=μ+σ⊙ε, ε∼N(0,I)',
['z: latent variable; x: observation','q(z|x): approximate posterior; p(z): prior','ELBO: evidence lower bound','⊙: elementwise multiplication; σ: positive standard deviations'],
['An encoder predicts parameters of a latent distribution from the input.','Sample standard noise ε, then transform it with μ and σ. This is the reparameterization trick.','A decoder models how latent values generate observations.','Maximize reconstruction log-likelihood minus KL to the prior, or minimize its negative.'],
[{q:'One-dimensional encoder gives μ=2, σ=0.5 and sampled ε=−1.',steps:['z=2+0.5×(−1).'],result:'Latent sample z=1.5.'},{q:'q=N(1,1) and prior p=N(0,1). Find KL(q‖p).',steps:['For a diagonal Gaussian and standard-normal prior: KL=½Σ(μ²+σ²−1−ln σ²).','Substitute: ½(1+1−1−0).'],result:'KL=0.5 nats.'}],
'The identity ln p(x)=ELBO+KL(q(z|x)‖p(z|x)) explains why ELBO is a lower bound: the final KL is nonnegative. Maximizing the bound improves a tractable objective but may leave an approximation gap. The reparameterization trick moves randomness into ε so gradients can flow through μ and σ. A deterministic autoencoder does not automatically define a usable sampling distribution over its codes.',
'A squared reconstruction error corresponds to a particular Gaussian decoder assumption and scaling; it is not universally interchangeable with every likelihood.',
'VAEs and approximate Bayesian methods combine latent representations, probability, integration and optimization.',
'If q exactly equals the prior, what is KL(q‖p)?',
'Zero. Equal distributions have no KL mismatch.',
'Variational inference trades an intractable posterior for an optimizable approximation.','network');
add('diffusion','10 · Generative mathematics','Diffusion, scores and the next learning path',
'Diffusion models learn to reverse a gradual corruption process. During training, known Gaussian noise is added to clean data. A neural network learns a denoising-related target. During generation, a sequence of learned reverse steps transforms noise into a sample. This connects probability, calculus, stochastic processes and numerical approximation.',
'xₜ=√ᾱₜ x₀+√(1−ᾱₜ)ε     Lsimple=E[‖ε−εθ(xₜ,t)‖²]     score(x)=∇x ln p(x)',
['x₀: clean data; xₜ: noisy data at time t','ᾱₜ: cumulative retained-signal factor between 0 and 1','ε: standard Gaussian noise; εθ: network noise prediction','score: gradient of log density, not a classification score'],
['Choose a noise schedule and sample a training time t.','Mix scaled clean data with independent Gaussian noise using the displayed formula.','Ask a time-conditioned network to predict the sampled noise under this common parameterization.','For generation, use a specified reverse sampler with its time schedule and update equations; a single noise subtraction is not the entire algorithm.'],
[{q:'Let x₀=2, ᾱₜ=0.64 and ε=−1.',steps:['Signal scale=√0.64=0.8.','Noise scale=√0.36=0.6.','xₜ=0.8×2+0.6×(−1).'],result:'Noisy value xₜ=1.'},{q:'For a standard normal density, find its score at x=2.',steps:['ln p(x)=constant−x²/2.','Differentiate with respect to x: score=−x.'],result:'Score at 2 is −2, pointing toward higher density.'}],
'With independent unit-variance signal and noise, the variance-preserving mixture has variance ᾱₜ+(1−ᾱₜ)=1. Different training weightings and parameterizations predict noise, clean data or velocity. Continuous-time treatments use stochastic differential equations and related probability-flow ordinary differential equations; numerical solvers introduce discretization error. For further depth, study measure-theoretic probability, stochastic calculus, optimal transport, information geometry and statistical learning bounds after mastering this syllabus.',
'The score of a noisy marginal distribution is not the score of one particular clean example. Deterministic and stochastic samplers use different reverse updates.',
'Diffusion is a modern synthesis of the foundations: vectors represent data, probability defines noise, derivatives define scores and optimization trains a denoiser.',
'If ᾱₜ=1, what is xₜ? If ᾱₜ=0, what is xₜ?',
'At 1: xₜ=x₀, all signal. At 0: xₜ=ε, all noise. These are mathematical endpoints; practical schedules may approach them.',
'Diffusion learns structured denoising across noise levels; advanced theory builds on the same mathematical foundations.','distribution');
const references=[
{title:'Mathematics for Machine Learning — Deisenroth, Faisal & Ong',url:'https://mml-book.github.io/',note:'Further reading: linear algebra, geometry, calculus, probability and optimization.'},
{title:'Deep Learning — Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville',url:'https://www.deeplearningbook.org/',note:'Further reading: numerical computation, networks, optimization and probabilistic models.'},
{title:'Stanford CS229 — course notes',url:'https://cs229.stanford.edu/main_notes.pdf',note:'Further reading: regression, kernels, generalization, EM and reinforcement learning.'},
{title:'Attention Is All You Need — original paper',url:'https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762',note:'Advanced reading for the attention lesson.'},
{title:'Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes — original paper',url:'https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114',note:'Advanced reading for variational autoencoders.'},
{title:'Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models — original paper',url:'https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.11239',note:'Advanced reading for the diffusion lesson.'}
];
lessons.forEach((lesson,i)=>{lesson.order=i+1;lesson.prerequisite=i?lessons[i-1].id:null;});
const data={lessons,references};
if(typeof module!=='undefined'&&module.exports)module.exports=data;
else root.ML_DATA=data;
})(typeof window!=='undefined'?window:globalThis);
